artifact.self_contained_problem:
A supernova (SN) is the explosive endpoint of certain stars, observable as a transient luminous event whose properties (light curves, spectra, ejecta composition, energetics, progenitor channel, and rates) depend on physical conditions of the progenitor system and its host environment. Consider the population of supernovae across cosmic history, parameterized by:

(i) metallicity Z of the progenitor and its environment;
(ii) stellar population (e.g., age distribution, initial mass function, binarity fraction) from which progenitors are drawn;
(iii) cosmic age / redshift z, i.e., the epoch in the history of the universe at which the explosion occurs;
(iv) host galaxy environment (galaxy type, local stellar density, gas content, star-formation rate, ISM properties).

Characterize quantitatively how each observable class of supernovae (e.g., Type Ia, Type II, stripped-envelope core-collapse types Ib/Ic, superluminous supernovae, etc.) and their physical properties — including explosion rates per unit stellar mass or star formation, peak luminosities, ejecta masses, nucleosynthetic yields, progenitor masses, and standardizable distance-indicator relations — vary as functions of (Z, stellar population, cosmic age, environment) across cosmic time. Furthermore, determine how these dependencies must be incorporated as systematic corrections, priors, or selection effects when supernovae are used as probes of cosmology, galactic chemical evolution, and large-scale structure of the universe.

